course: dictionary
lesson: 5877
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Mangle is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it, because they are its context and they give clues.
explain: «Put yourself through your mangle, washerwoman, and iron your face and crimp it, and you'll pass for quite a decent-looking Toad!» Here a mangle is a machine with rollers that squeezes water out of wet washing. The clues are washerwoman and iron, which both belong to washing.
explain: «The bike was mangled in the crash.» Now the clue is crash. A mangled bike is crushed, torn and badly out of shape. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: does «The bike was crushed and out of shape in the crash» still make sense? It does, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Mangle can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action word).
fact: A mangle is an old machine with two rollers, turned by a handle, that squeezed water out of wet washing.
fact: To mangle something means to crush, tear or damage it badly, so it is out of shape.
fact: To mangle words, music or a story means to spoil them by doing them badly.
fact: Machines can mangle fingers, so keep your hands away from moving parts.
exercise: «Before washing machines, clothes were squeezed through a mangle with two rollers.» What is a mangle? | A heavy iron for pressing shirts | *A machine with rollers for squeezing water out of wet washing | A washing board for scrubbing | A big tub for boiling clothes
exercise: «He mangled the French phrase.» What does mangled mean here? | Pronounced it perfectly | *Said it badly, spoiling the words | Wrote it in gold ink | Whispered it very softly
exercise: «The bike was mangled in the crash.» What does mangled mean? | Painted a bright colour | *Crushed and badly damaged | Shiny and brand new | Folded up neatly for storage
test: Why should you keep your hands away from the moving parts of a machine? | Because they make the machine sing | Because they help the machine to move faster | Because they keep your hands warm | *Because they could mangle your fingers
test: «Monday was wash day: the clothes were boiled, scrubbed and squeezed through a mangle.» What is a mangle in this sentence? | A kind of soap | A type of bread | A sort of hat | *A machine with rollers for squeezing water out
exam: The toy robot was mangled when the lorry rolled over it. What does mangled mean? | Turned on and dancing | Shining and clean | Hidden under the bed | *Crushed and out of shape
